WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.818
When applications call Ollama, they should treat it as a service boundary.

2
00:00:04.818 --> 00:00:12.083
It has request behavior, latency, errors, model availability, capacity limits, and operational assumptions.

3
00:00:12.083 --> 00:00:21.642
Even if the service is running locally, it can still be unavailable, slow, overloaded, misconfigured, or missing the model an application expects.

4
00:00:21.642 --> 00:00:30.818
API clients need timeouts, error handling, predictable model selection, and clear assumptions about streaming or non-streaming behavior.

5
00:00:30.818 --> 00:00:39.766
A tool that works during a manual test may behave differently when multiple users are active or when a model needs to load before responding.

6
00:00:39.766 --> 00:00:47.566
Applications should avoid fragile assumptions about response timing, model availability, or unlimited concurrency.

7
00:00:47.566 --> 00:00:53.913
Teams should decide how model names, prompts, options, and user context are passed to the service.

8
00:00:53.913 --> 00:01:00.566
If a model name is hardcoded in several applications, replacing or retiring that model becomes harder.

9
00:01:00.566 --> 00:01:07.525
If prompt options are scattered across clients, support teams may not know which behavior users are seeing.

10
00:01:07.525 --> 00:01:12.878
A stable integration layer can make model selection and defaults easier to manage.

11
00:01:12.878 --> 00:01:19.378
Local APIs still need operational discipline when they support shared tools or business workflows.

12
00:01:19.378 --> 00:01:27.255
Clear integration boundaries help separate application bugs from runtime, model, storage, and infrastructure issues.

13
00:01:27.255 --> 00:01:35.667
They also give operators a place to document expectations for timeouts, retries, request size, streaming, and failure behavior.

14
00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:40.791
Application teams should also decide what user context is appropriate to send.

15
00:01:40.791 --> 00:01:46.679
A local service can still receive sensitive prompts, file excerpts, or retrieved context.

16
00:01:46.679 --> 00:01:55.550
Integration code should keep data handling intentional, avoid unnecessary logging, and make it clear which workflow owns each request.
